A column becomes NOT NULL with a DEFAULT, over rows that hold NULL there.
The copy names the column, so its NULL is copied and the DEFAULT never
applies: refused, and the error says why and what to do.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'soup', NULL), (2, 'bread', 'warm');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '') STRICT;

== plan
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '') STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name, note
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name", "note") SELECT "id", "name", "note" FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"

== error
migrating test.db: table `dish`: its rows, by id, name, note: NOT NULL constraint failed: dish.note (a NOT NULL column needs a value in every row: a column new to the table takes its DEFAULT, a copied one keeps its NULLs)
